{
 "id": "it-is-well",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "It Is Well with My Soul",
 "description": "Philip Bliss's hymn as he harmonised it in 1876, for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse and the chorus. In the chorus the tune holds \"well\" and \"soul\" while the other parts answer \"it is well\" beneath it, then all four sing the last line together. Steady and warm.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "hymn",
  "gospel"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 92,
 "key": "C",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Horatio G. Spafford (1873)",
  "tune": "Philip P. Bliss, \"Ville du Havre\" (1876)",
  "arrangement": "Philip P. Bliss (1876), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Spafford died 1888",
  "tune": "public domain: Bliss died 1876",
  "arrangement": "Bliss's four parts (public domain), with breaths written in for beginners and the alto and tenor revoiced so 2 and 3 singers keep each chord's third. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): Bliss's own bass, as the 1876 scan prints it: in the first line it leaps up a fifth to G on \"riv-\" and down a fifth to its low G on \"eth\"; at both \"it is well with my soul\" cadences it drops an octave, G to low G, from \"well\" to \"with\", the same note under the cadence; and on \"rows,\" (\"When sor-rows,\") his bass and the tune both sing B, the leading tone, under a diminished seventh whose D and A flat the alto and tenor take.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "P. P. Bliss and Ira D. Sankey, Gospel Hymns No. 2 (Biglow & Main, New York; John Church & Co., Cincinnati, 1876), No. 76, p. 78, in Db and written here in C; the chorus's held \"well\" and \"soul\" read as tied; read from the scan at archive.org/details/hymnsno02blis/page/n81"
 },
